---
<Page
  current="home"
  title="abap2UI5 - why"
  description="abap2UI5 for decision makers: what you can build with it, who runs it, where it is the shorter path to a UI5 app on SAP and where it is not, what it costs and how to start with one real screen."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div>
        <h1>UI5 apps for ABAP,<br>built by <em>the team you already have</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">An app is a regular ABAP class, nothing more: written in ADT or SE80, checked by ATC, carried by a transport, as every ABAP developer does it today. <b>The business logic stays in ABAP, next to the data: no JavaScript, no extra platform, no license fee.</b> Open source since 2023, in production on the desktop and on the warehouse floor.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="composite">
        <img src={`${base}apps/hero-class-and-phone.webp`} alt="One ABAP class in the ABAP development tools, and the app it produces on a handheld: a table of sales orders with status and amount" width="1800" height="820" fetchpriority="high">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Stats />

  <section class="section" id="the-problem-it-answers" aria-labelledby="h-build">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What you can build</p>
      <h2 id="h-build">Every process in SAP that needs a screen</h2>
      <p class="intro">Whatever runs on paper, in Excel or in SAP GUI today becomes a browser app, built in your own system by your own team. Each card opens a running example.</p>
      <div class="cards four">
        {useCases.map(([icon, h, t, cls, alt]) => (
          <div class="card shot">
            <a class="shot-img" href={`https://abap2ui5.github.io/samples-site/sample/${cls}/`}><img src={`${base}apps/${cls}.webp`} alt={alt} loading="lazy"></a>
            <Icon name={icon} /><h3>{h}</h3><p>{t}</p>
            <a class="shot-link" href={`https://abap2ui5.github.io/samples-site/sample/${cls}/`}>See the example app</a>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <h3 class="sub">One app, every device: no build per device</h3>
      <p class="intro">The same class, opened on a phone, a tablet and a desktop. It runs in the browser on iOS, Android and Windows, on rugged industrial handhelds too; nothing to install, nothing to distribute, and the layout adapts on its own.</p>
      <div class="devices">
        <figure class="device phone"><div class="screen"><img src={`${base}apps/device-phone.webp`} alt="The sales orders app on a phone: one order per row, region and status folded under it" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption><b>Phone and handheld scanner</b><span>Picking, counting, approving at the shelf or on the way. The scanner's input lands in the field, no tap needed.</span></figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="device tablet"><div class="screen"><img src={`${base}apps/device-tablet.webp`} alt="The sales orders app on a tablet: the full table" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption><b>Tablet</b><span>At the machine, in the workshop, at the goods receipt: the full table, touch-sized.</span></figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="device desktop"><div class="screen"><img src={`${base}apps/device-desktop.webp`} alt="The sales orders app on a desktop: the full table with room to spare" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption><b>Desktop</b><span>The worklist and the cockpit at the desk, keyboard-driven, in the launchpad next to the Fiori apps.</span></figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <p class="more">→ Browse the <a href="https://abap2ui5.github.io/samples-site/">example apps</a> for the screen closest to yours: hundreds of them, each running in the browser and maintained with the framework. Or read <a href={`${DOCS}get_started/about`}>In a Nutshell</a> for how it works.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt" id="when-to-use-it-and-when-not" aria-labelledby="h-fit">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Compared with the market</p>
      <h2 id="h-fit">Where it is the shorter path, and where it is not</h2>
      <p class="intro">Four ways to get a UI5 app on SAP, side by side with abap2UI5: SAP's standard route, a custom UI5 app, and the two kinds of commercial tool sold for it. No vendor is named: offers and prices change, and a named comparison ages; each column describes what you would buy. Rows marked with a dash are where abap2UI5 is not ahead.</p>
      <dl class="market">
        {market.map(([h, t]) => <div><dt>{h}</dt><dd>{t}</dd></div>)}
      </dl>
      <div class="table compare">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col" class="us">abap2UI5</th>{rivals.map((r) => <th scope="col">{r}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>{compare.map(([k, a, b, c, d, e, even]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{k}</th>
              <td class={even ? "us even" : "us"} data-th="abap2UI5">{a}</td>
              {[b, c, d, e].map((t, i) => <td data-th={rivals[i]}>{t}</td>)}
            </tr>
          ))}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="fit">
        <div class="yes">
          <h3>Where teams choose abap2UI5</h3>
          <ul class="reasons">{useIt.map(([b, t]) => <li><b>{b}</b>{t}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
        <div class="no">
          <h3>Where something else fits better</h3>
          <ul class="reasons">{notIt.map(([b, t]) => <li><b>{b}</b>{t}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="more">→ <a href={`${DOCS}advanced/insights/30-on-stack-or-side-by-side`}>On-Stack or Side-by-Side</a> and <a href={`${DOCS}advanced/insights/31-one-app-many-systems`}>One App, Many Systems</a> are the deployment scenarios in detail.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="what-you-keep" aria-labelledby="h-keep">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Enterprise readiness</p>
      <h2 id="h-keep">Ready for your enterprise: inside the security, the process and the release cycle you already have</h2>
      <p class="intro">The logic stays in ABAP; logon, authorizations and data stay SAP's. One ABAP class per app, the business logic in it, no generated artifacts, no tables that describe a screen. Nothing of the app runs in the browser, so there is no client logic to secure. Every rule that applies to ABAP, from code review to the four-eyes principle on transports, applies to an app unchanged.</p>
      <div class="table">
        <table>
