  <section class="section" id="where-it-runs" aria-labelledby="h-runs">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Where it runs</p>
      <h2 id="h-runs">On the SAP system you already have, and nowhere else</h2>
      <p class="intro">The browser loads the standard UI5 library; the app, its logic and its data stay on your SAP system. No middleware, no extra server, no frontend deployment.</p>
      <div class="split">
        <dl class="facts-list">
          <dt>Every SAP operating model, from one source</dt>
          <dd>
            <div class="models-group hot">
              <h4>ABAP Cloud <small>clean core, released APIs only: abap2UI5 passes the checks</small></h4>
              <ul class="models">
                <li><b>SAP S/4HANA Cloud Public Edition</b><span>Clean core is mandatory; the same class runs, no rewrite on the way up.</span></li>
                <li><b>SAP BTP ABAP Environment</b><span>The standalone ABAP system on BTP: for apps beside the core, or as the side-by-side system below.</span></li>
                <li><b>S/4HANA Private Cloud and on-premise, in ABAP Cloud</b><span>Developing in the ABAP Cloud language version on a RISE or on-premise system: abap2UI5 fits the clean-core tier there too.</span></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="models-group">
              <h4>Standard ABAP <small>the classic language version, every release</small></h4>
              <ul class="models">
                <li><b>SAP S/4HANA Cloud Private Edition</b><span>RISE with SAP, in SAP's or a hyperscaler's data centre.</span></li>
                <li><b>SAP S/4HANA on-premise</b><span>In your own data centre or at a hoster.</span></li>
                <li><b>SAP ECC and NetWeaver 7.02 to 7.5x</b><span>Where RAP and a Fiori stack are out of reach, and an app written today moves to S/4HANA unchanged.</span></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </dd>
          <dt>Two ways to deploy</dt>
          <dd>
            <ul class="models">
              <li><b>On-stack</b><span>abap2UI5 on the system where the data and the logic live. An app calls BAPIs, function modules or RAP actions directly, as any program there does. The usual setup, one abapGit pull.</span></li>
              <li><b>Side-by-side</b><span>abap2UI5 on a separate ABAP system, the BTP ABAP Environment or a hub, and the apps reach the core over RFC or HTTP. For a core kept clean of custom UI, or one UI layer in front of several systems.</span></li>
            </ul>
          </dd>
          <dt>Where an app appears</dt>
          <dd><ul class="chips"><li>Browser tab</li><li>Fiori launchpad tile</li><li>SAP Build Work Zone</li><li>Microsoft Teams</li><li>Microsoft Excel</li><li>iOS &amp; Android</li><li>Inside your own UI5 app</li></ul></dd>
          <dt>Without internet access</dt>
          <dd>A productive installation serves UI5 from the system itself, so nothing is loaded from outside. Whether a productive on-premise system may load UI5 from SAP's public CDN is SAP's rule, not the project's (SAP Note 2943781); serving it from the system settles the question.</dd>
        </dl>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 class="first">What installation takes</h3>
          <ol class="steps-basis">
            <li>Pull the package with abapGit, as for any open-source ABAP</li>
            <li>Create one service endpoint and put an authorization on it</li>
            <li>Set the four production defaults: UI5 source, error details, content security policy, endpoint authorization</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="after">No job to schedule, no extra server, no schema to migrate on upgrade.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="more">→ <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/productive_usage`}>Productive Usage</a>, <a href={`${DOCS}configuration/ui5_versions`}>UI5 Versions</a> and <a href={`${DOCS}integration/`}>Integration</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

